From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Wed, 10 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 81/81] local: mailbox: qcom-ipcc: re-arm receive signals after
 hibernation

After a cross-boot hibernate resume on x1e80100 the ADSP glink edge is dead:
battery, charging, thermal reporting, audio and UCSI all time out, and every
attempt to recover it at the protocol level failed because the remote never
appeared to answer.

It answers. Linux is not hearing it.

IPCC_REG_RECV_SIGNAL_ENABLE is written only by qcom_ipcc_unmask_irq(), and
the reboot clears it. For an ordinary interrupt that does not matter:
suspend_device_irqs() masks it, resume_device_irqs() unmasks it, and the
unmask rewrites the register. The glink edge interrupt is not ordinary --
qcom_glink_smem requests it with IRQF_NO_SUSPEND:

  drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c:
      ret = devm_request_irq(&smem->dev, smem->irq, qcom_glink_smem_intr,
                             IRQF_NO_SUSPEND | IRQF_NO_AUTOEN, ...

so it is never masked at suspend and never unmasked at resume. Nothing
rewrites RECV_SIGNAL_ENABLE, and every doorbell the remote rings afterwards
is dropped in the IPCC block.

Measured on a resumed machine with the qcom_glink tracepoints armed: zero
inbound interrupts on both glink-smem lines over an idle baseline, and our
own cmd_version tx as the only glink event in the capture. Sending still
works, because IPCC_REG_SEND_ID is a stateless write -- a GLINK SSR
do_cleanup reached the ADSP and made its audio service tear down
(18x "qcom,apr GPR: Port not registered"). Only the receive direction is
dead, which is exactly the asymmetry this bug produces.

The DSP had not crashed: smp2p fatal/ready/handover/stop all read zero and
remoteproc still reported it attached.

Replay the enable for every mapped IPCC interrupt that is not masked, from
.restore_noirq so the signals are live before resume_device_irqs() runs.

This is the fourth instance of the same defect on this platform, after the
SMMU, the SPMI PMIC irqchip and TLMM: hardware state programmed once at
request or probe time, lost to the reboot, and never replayed because the
driver's software state came back from the hibernation image believing it was
still there.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/mailbox/qcom-ipcc.c | 50 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 50 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/mailbox/qcom-ipcc.c
+++ b/drivers/mailbox/qcom-ipcc.c
@@ -255,6 +255,55 @@
 	return devm_mbox_controller_register(dev, mbox);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Hibernate restore: re-arm the receive signals.
+ *
+ * IPCC_REG_RECV_SIGNAL_ENABLE is written only by qcom_ipcc_unmask_irq(), and
+ * the reboot clears it. For an ordinary interrupt that is harmless, because
+ * suspend_device_irqs() masks it and resume_device_irqs() unmasks it again,
+ * which rewrites the register on the way back.
+ *
+ * The glink edge interrupt is not ordinary. qcom_glink_smem requests it with
+ * IRQF_NO_SUSPEND, so it is never masked at suspend and never unmasked at
+ * resume -- nothing rewrites RECV_SIGNAL_ENABLE, and after a cross-boot
+ * hibernate resume every doorbell the remote rings is silently dropped.
+ *
+ * Observed on x1e80100: after a resume, zero inbound interrupts on both
+ * glink-smem lines while the remote is demonstrably alive and receiving --
+ * a GLINK SSR do_cleanup reached the ADSP and made its audio service tear
+ * down, and a fresh VERSION went out on the wire. Sending works because
+ * IPCC_REG_SEND_ID is a stateless write; only the receive direction is dead.
+ * Everything downstream (battery, charging, thermal, audio, UCSI) then times
+ * out, and no amount of protocol-level recovery can help, because the replies
+ * never arrive.
+ *
+ * Replay the enable for every mapped IPCC interrupt that is not masked.
+ * .restore_noirq, so the signals are live before resume_device_irqs() runs.
+ */
+static int qcom_ipcc_restore_noirq(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct qcom_ipcc *ipcc = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	unsigned int nr = irq_get_nr_irqs();
+	unsigned int count = 0;
+	unsigned int virq;
+
+	for (virq = 0; virq < nr; virq++) {
+		struct irq_data *d = irq_get_irq_data(virq);
+
+		if (!d || d->domain != ipcc->irq_domain)
+			continue;
+		if (irqd_irq_disabled(d) || irqd_irq_masked(d))
+			continue;
+
+		writel(irqd_to_hwirq(d), ipcc->base + IPCC_REG_RECV_SIGNAL_ENABLE);
+		count++;
+	}
+
+	dev_info(dev, "re-armed %u receive signal(s)\n", count);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int qcom_ipcc_pm_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct qcom_ipcc *ipcc = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
@@ -357,6 +406,7 @@
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops qcom_ipcc_dev_pm_ops = {
 	NOIRQ_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(NULL, qcom_ipcc_pm_resume)
+	.restore_noirq = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_ipcc_restore_noirq),
 };
 
 static struct platform_driver qcom_ipcc_driver = {
